Title: "Integers"
Id: "3919403c-8205-49f9-aebb-fc23b59961ae"


Text: "The natural numbers can be added but not freely subtracted: there is no
       natural number equal to $3 - 5$. The integers repair this by adjoining
       negative numbers. The classical construction, carried out here,
       represents each integer as a difference of two naturals -- the pair
       $(n, m)$ standing for $n - m$ -- and then identifies pairs that ought to
       name the same difference. This is exactly a quotient by an equivalence
       relation, so it draws on every earlier chapter at once: the naturals,
       their addition, the Cartesian product, and the quotient of a set by a
       relation."
Id: "6ab727ff-4d3c-467a-a090-23baa9056340"


Text: "Two pairs $(n_1, m_1)$ and $(n_2, m_2)$ represent the same integer when
       $n_1 - m_1 = n_2 - m_2$. Since subtraction is not yet available, this is
       phrased using addition alone, as $n_1 + m_2 = n_2 + m_1$. The relation so
       defined is an equivalence relation on pairs of naturals."
Id: "86f693bc-1d69-4bfd-bef4-c67033b73acb"


[\integer.equivalence]
Declares: R is \equivalence.relation:on{\naturals.set \.set.cross./ \naturals.set}
expresses:
. let: (n1, m1), (n2, m2) "in" (.\naturals.set \.set.cross./ \naturals.set.)
  then:
  . have: R( (n1, m1), (n2, m2) )
    iff: n1 + m2 = n2 + m1
Documented:
. called: "integer equivalence relation"
. written: "\sim_{\mathbb{Z}}"
. description: "The relation on pairs of naturals that identifies $(n_1, m_1)$
                with $(n_2, m_2)$ exactly when $n_1 + m_2 = n_2 + m_1$. This is
                the cross-multiplied form of $n_1 - m_1 = n_2 - m_2$, expressed
                with addition alone, and it is an equivalence relation."
Id: "ee34451f-a1b6-4339-9ec4-f260c0a4d395"


Text: "The set of integers is then the quotient of the pairs of naturals by this
       relation. Each integer is an equivalence class of differences, so that
       $2 - 5$ and $0 - 3$ name the same integer, written $-3$. With
       $\mathbb{Z}$ in hand, subtraction becomes total and the arithmetic of the
       naturals extends to negative numbers."
Id: "7dedd212-2822-4340-bab5-11991187596f"


[\integers.set]
Declares: Z := (\naturals.set \.set.cross./ \naturals.set) \.mod./ \integer.equivalence is \set
Documented:
. called: "integers"
. written: "\mathbb{Z}"
. description: "The set $\mathbb{Z}$ of integers, constructed as the quotient of
                $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ by the integer equivalence
                relation. Each integer is a class of pairs $(n, m)$ representing
                the difference $n - m$, so $\mathbb{Z}$ contains the naturals
                together with their negatives."
Id: "2879813a-8202-4d27-b248-83687c132e84"
